# Independently authored PG printf-style format() bug
# sentinel slice for the GPL MTR-format runner. NOT
# copied or adapted from MySQL's mysql-test/.
#
# Existing coverage (read carefully — DO NOT duplicate):
#   - MySQL `FORMAT(number, decimals[, locale])` (thousands-
#     separator number formatter): pinned exhaustively in
#     format_basic_extra.test (arity, rounding, locale,
#     decimals clamp, NULL passthrough, non-finite f64).
#     That slice covers the NUMERIC form only and never
#     touches a printf-style format string.
#   - `format_type(23, NULL)`: pinned at
#     pg_ordinality_match_reflection_sentinel_basic_extra
#     .test:236. Different function (one_arg/two_arg type-
#     name formatter, not the printf one).
#
# Genuinely ZERO prior coverage on these shapes (grep
# verified across tests/main/*.test — `format\(`,
# `SELECT format`, `EXECUTE format`):
#   - PG `format(fmtstr, args...)` printf-style with `%s`,
#     `%I`, `%L`, `%%`, positional `%1$s`. None of the
#     spec characters have ever been pinned.
#   - The `EXECUTE format('SELECT * FROM %I WHERE c = %L',
#     ...)` dynamic-SQL escape idiom (canonical PG ORM /
#     pgPLSQL / Debezium pattern).
#
# Pre-existing pattern fingerprints this slice joins:
#   - CORRUPTION-class family: PR #421 (DEFAULT subquery
#     stored as text literal), PR #432 (TIMESTAMP(n)
#     precision silent-drop), PR #438 (EXPLAIN option
#     modifiers FORMAT/ANALYZE/BUFFERS silent-drop).
#     Shared mechanism: the wire reports success, the
#     value/output is structurally a STRING but its
#     content is WRONG. No error, no warning. Downstream
#     parsers / SQL re-executors blow up far from the
#     cause.
#
# THE BUG (CORRUPTION class):
#
#   PG's `format(fmtstr, args...)` is the printf-style
#   string interpolation function. Specs:
#     %s — string-coerce the arg
#     %I — quote_ident()       (table/column names)
#     %L — quote_literal()     (string literals)
#     %% — literal `%`
#     %1$s — positional reference to arg 1
#
#   In zeta, `format(fmtstr, arg, ...)` is silently
#   re-routed to the MySQL `FORMAT(number, decimals)`
#   function. The fmtstr coerces to number `0`, the arg
#   coerces to decimals `0`, and the call returns the
#   string `'0'` for EVERY printf-style invocation.
#
#   Mechanism proof (pinned below as Shape M1/M2):
#     - `format(0, 0)` → '0'          (the route)
#     - `format(1234.5678, 2)` → '1,234.57'
#       (proves zeta is running the MySQL numeric
#        FORMAT() function — note the comma separator
#        and 2-decimal rounding).
#
# Why this is CORRUPTION-class (joins PR #421/#432/#438):
#
#   - Wrong content, NO error signal. The wire returns
#     the literal string "0" — not even NULL or empty.
#   - Apps use `format()` for SQL-escape in dynamic SQL:
#       EXECUTE format('SELECT * FROM %I WHERE c = %L',
#                      tbl_name, val);
#     With this bug, the inner string becomes "0", so
#     `EXECUTE` runs `0` as SQL — either a parse error
#     far from the cause, or (worse) some accidental
#     match if "0" happens to be a valid statement
#     fragment elsewhere.
#   - CDC / replication pipelines that generate INSERT
#     statements via format(): silent data loss. Every
#     row formatted to the string "0".
#   - ORM raw-mode SQL builders (Django RawQuery,
#     SQLAlchemy text() with format) emit garbage.
#
# Wire divergence check: the function dispatch table is
# shared across pgwire and mysqlwire (both wires reach
# the same `format` builtin in zeta-executor). The
# silent reroute to MySQL FORMAT happens in the function
# resolver, not in either wire layer. Expected identical
# behavior on both wires.
#
# Adaptation notes:
#   # zeta-adapt: CORRUPTION-class BUG — PG printf-
#     style `format(fmtstr, args...)` is silently
#     re-routed to MySQL `FORMAT(number, decimals)`.
#     Every printf-style call (with `%s`, `%I`, `%L`,
#     `%%`, `%1$s`) returns the literal string "0"
#     regardless of the format string and args. NO
#     error, NO warning. Workaround: do NOT call
#     printf-style format() against zeta. Build
#     dynamic SQL via string concatenation with
#     manual quote_ident()/quote_literal() (if
#     available) or via bind parameters in the
#     prepared-statement protocol. EXECUTE format(...)
#     dynamic SQL inside DO blocks / functions will
#     run the literal string "0" as SQL.
#   # zeta-adapt: single-arg form `format(fmtstr)`
#     errors 1105 with the MySQL message "FORMAT
#     requires 2 or 3 arguments (number, decimals
#     [, locale])". The error message text itself is
#     the clearest tell that the reroute is to the
#     MySQL numeric FORMAT, not the PG printf one.
#   # zeta-adapt: `format('%s', NULL)` (and similar
#     fmtstr-plus-NULL shapes) returns SQL NULL (NULL
#     coerces to decimals=NULL, which short-circuits
#     FORMAT() to NULL). Different output from the
#     all-non-NULL shapes (which return "0") but
#     equally wrong — neither matches PG semantics
#     (PG: `%s` formats NULL as the literal `'<NULL>'`).
#
# This slice pins (TEN shapes — eight CORRUPTION shapes,
# two mechanism-proof shapes):
#
#   CORRUPTION-class BUG — printf-style format() returns
#   "0" regardless of args (headline):
#
#     Shape A — `format('hi %s', 'world')` → '0'
#       (real PG: 'hi world'). Basic %s interpolation,
#       single arg.
#
#     Shape B — `format('val=%I', 'my_ident')` → '0'
#       (real PG: 'val="my_ident"'). %I is supposed to
#       quote_ident() the arg.
#
#     Shape C — `format('val=%L', 'literal')` → '0'
#       (real PG: 'val=''literal'''). %L is supposed to
#       quote_literal() the arg.
#
#     Shape D — `format('val=%s, num=%s', 'x', 42)` →
#       '0' (real PG: 'val=x, num=42'). Two args, mixed
#       types.
#
#     Shape E — `format('SELECT * FROM %I WHERE c =
#       %L', 'users', 'alice')` → '0' (real PG:
#       'SELECT * FROM "users" WHERE c = ''alice''').
#       The headline EXECUTE format() dynamic-SQL
#       idiom. If wrapped in EXECUTE, runs the string
#       '0' as SQL.
#
#     Shape F — `format('%I.%I', 'public', 'users')`
#       → '0' (real PG: '"public"."users"'). Two
#       %I specs back-to-back, qualified identifier.
#
#     Shape G — `format('%1$s-%1$s', 'x')` → '0'
#       (real PG: 'x-x'). Positional reference,
#       same arg twice.
#
#     Shape H — `format('%s', 'only')` → '0'
#       (real PG: 'only'). Single spec, single arg —
#       the minimal printf shape.
#
#   MECHANISM-PROOF pins — show WHY the result is "0":
#
#     Shape M1 — `format(0, 0)` → '0'. Numeric
#       FORMAT(0, 0) returns the literal string '0'.
#       This is the exact code path every shape above
#       is hitting after the silent reroute (fmtstr
#       string coerces to number 0, arg coerces to
#       decimals 0).
#
#     Shape M2 — `format(1234.5678, 2)` → '1,234.57'.
#       Decisively proves the reroute: thousand-
#       separator comma and 2-decimal rounding are the
#       fingerprint of MySQL FORMAT(number, decimals).
#       PG's printf format() would treat the first arg
#       as a fmtstr and error / return the literal.
#
#   PARSE/ARITY witness pins:
#
#     Shape P1 — `format('plain string')` (no args
#       beyond fmtstr) errors 1105 "FORMAT requires 2
#       or 3 arguments (number, decimals[, locale])".
#       The error MESSAGE TEXT itself is the clearest
#       proof of the reroute — the words "number,
#       decimals[, locale]" come from MySQL FORMAT,
#       NOT PG printf format. PG accepts single-arg
#       format() (the fmtstr passes through unchanged).
#
#     Shape P2 — `format('%s', NULL)` → SQL NULL.
#       Different output from the all-non-NULL shapes
#       (which return '0'), because NULL coerces to
#       decimals=NULL, which short-circuits FORMAT()
#       to NULL. Pin the divergence to lock it in —
#       apps that happen to pass NULL through %s will
#       see SQL NULL instead of '0' (and instead of
#       PG's literal '<NULL>' string).

--echo === Shape A: format('hi %s', 'world') — returns '0' instead of 'hi world' (CORRUPTION) ===

# Headline BUG. Real PG: 'hi world'. zeta: '0'.

SELECT format('hi %s', 'world') AS basic_s;

--echo === Shape B: format('val=%I', 'my_ident') — quote_ident dropped, returns '0' ===

# Real PG: 'val="my_ident"' (%I wraps the arg in
# double-quotes per quote_ident() rules). zeta: '0'.

SELECT format('val=%I', 'my_ident') AS quote_ident_spec;

--echo === Shape C: format('val=%L', 'literal') — quote_literal dropped, returns '0' ===

# Real PG: 'val=''literal''' (%L wraps the arg in
# single-quotes per quote_literal() rules — the SQL
# escape used for embedding values in dynamic SQL).
# zeta: '0'.

SELECT format('val=%L', 'literal') AS quote_literal_spec;

--echo === Shape D: format('val=%s, num=%s', 'x', 42) — multi-arg %s, returns '0' ===

# Real PG: 'val=x, num=42' (two %s specs, mixed string
# and integer args). zeta: '0'.

SELECT format('val=%s, num=%s', 'x', 42) AS two_args_mixed;

--echo === Shape E: format('SELECT * FROM %I WHERE c = %L', 'users', 'alice') — dynamic SQL, returns '0' (CORRUPTION HEADLINE) ===

# The canonical PG dynamic-SQL escape pattern. Real PG:
# 'SELECT * FROM "users" WHERE c = ''alice'''. Apps wrap
# this in EXECUTE inside DO blocks / functions to build
# safely-quoted dynamic SQL at runtime. zeta returns
# '0', so EXECUTE format(...) runs the literal '0' as
# SQL — either a parse error far from the cause, or
# (worse) a silent semantic divergence.

SELECT format('SELECT * FROM %I WHERE c = %L', 'users', 'alice') AS dynamic_sql_idiom;

--echo === Shape F: format('%I.%I', 'public', 'users') — qualified identifier, returns '0' ===

# Real PG: '"public"."users"'. Two %I specs back-to-
# back is the schema-qualified-name pattern. zeta: '0'.

SELECT format('%I.%I', 'public', 'users') AS qualified_ident;

--echo === Shape G: format('%1$s-%1$s', 'x') — positional reference, returns '0' ===

# Real PG: 'x-x' (%1$s is the positional form that
# references arg 1; using it twice prints the same arg
# twice). zeta: '0'.

SELECT format('%1$s-%1$s', 'x') AS positional_ref;

--echo === Shape H: format('%s', 'only') — minimal printf shape, returns '0' ===

# Real PG: 'only'. The smallest possible printf-style
# call: one spec, one arg. zeta: '0'.

SELECT format('%s', 'only') AS minimal_printf;

--echo === Shape M1: format(0, 0) — mechanism proof, returns '0' ===

# This is the numeric FORMAT(number, decimals) code
# path every printf-style shape above ends up hitting
# after the silent reroute. The fmtstr ('hi %s' etc.)
# coerces to number 0; the arg coerces to decimals 0;
# FORMAT(0, 0) returns the literal string '0'.

SELECT format(0, 0) AS numeric_zero_zero;

--echo === Shape M2: format(1234.5678, 2) — mechanism proof, '1,234.57' (MySQL numeric FORMAT fingerprint) ===

# Decisive proof of the silent reroute: the comma
# thousand-separator and 2-decimal rounding are the
# unique fingerprint of MySQL's FORMAT(number, decimals).
# PG's printf format() would treat '1234.5678' as a
# fmtstr and pass it through unchanged (no specs to
# expand). zeta's '1,234.57' result conclusively
# identifies which function is actually being called.

SELECT format(1234.5678, 2) AS numeric_decimal_proof;

--echo === Shape P1: format('plain string') single-arg — errors 1105 with MySQL message ===

# The error MESSAGE TEXT is the clearest proof of the
# silent reroute. PG's printf format() accepts a single
# argument (the fmtstr) and returns it unchanged. zeta
# requires 2-or-3 args because it has wired format() to
# the numeric MySQL FORMAT(number, decimals[, locale]).
# The words "number, decimals[, locale]" in the error
# can ONLY come from the MySQL numeric impl — they have
# no meaning in the PG printf one.

--error 1105
SELECT format('plain string');

--echo === Shape P2: format('%s', NULL) — returns SQL NULL (not '0', not '<NULL>' literal) ===

# Divergent output from Shapes A-H: NULL coerces to
# decimals=NULL, which short-circuits FORMAT() to
# return SQL NULL. Real PG: '<NULL>' literal (the
# printf format() treats NULL args as the literal
# four-character string '<NULL>'). Pin the zeta
# divergence to lock it in.

SELECT format('%s', NULL) AS fmt_spec_with_null_arg;
